{#- Managed by Ansible (remote-devbox). Bound the tmpfs behind /tmp.

    Everything written to /tmp is resident memory that can only be swapped, never
    dropped, and the vendor unit sizes it at half of RAM — 31 GiB here, which is not a
    bound at all. Measured 2026-08-20: 7.4 GiB of /tmp, 6.0 GiB of it a scratch checkout
    an agent invented on the spot, charged as unreclaimable shmem against the agent
    host's cgroup and helping pin it on its memory ceiling for over an hour.

    The neighbouring tmp.conf already routes agents to a TMPDIR on disk and already
    explains why retention cannot help: the damage lands in minutes, the policy measures
    days. What was still missing is a wall. Chasing writers does not work either — that
    6.0 GiB tree matched no committed script, so the next agent simply invents a new
    path. Bounding the filesystem is the part that holds regardless of who writes.

    Over the wall a job gets ENOSPC and fails alone, which is the same bargain the
    memory ceilings make: the runaway dies instead of the box.

    Options= REPLACES the vendor option set rather than extending it, so every option
    the vendor unit carried is restated here. Dropping nosuid/nodev off a world-writable
    directory would be a security regression, not a tuning choice. Kept verbatim from
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/tmp.mount with size= as the only change. #}
{%- set raw_size = devbox_host.tmp_size | default('4G', true) -%}
{#- A percentage is legal here, but systemd reads % as a specifier escape, so it has to
    be doubled in the unit file or the mount silently gets a malformed size. -#}
{%- set size = raw_size | replace('%', '%%') -%}
[Mount]
Options=mode=1777,strictatime,nosuid,nodev,size={{ size }},nr_inodes=1m,x-systemd.graceful-option=usrquota
